<BeforeAfter />

For years my computers had tons of problems I didn't understand. Games would stutter, things would
lag, I just didn't have any idea what was going on, and if I restarted, magically sometimes things
would be fixed. This time I couldn't restart, so Opus 5.5 and I had to find the actual problems: my
wife's movie was being transcoded on the GPU, an NVIDIA Overlay process was sitting on 12.7 GB of
memory, and two Razer services had left 32,709 zombie processes behind. Fixing those took Subnautica
2 from 2 FPS back to 60.

<Story>

<Step is="server" />

## A server that sometimes plays games

I started self hosting because of Immich, and I don't have a server with a good GPU, so my laptop
(a Legion 5 with a 3070) became the server. These days the same laptop also runs Jellyfin, sits on
top of the TV unit with a gajillion wires going into the back, and never gets turned off.

After a couple days of being on, I realized I was getting really bad performance. This was back when
I was using Cursor, and with Cursor I figured out that Nahimic (the audio "enhancer" Lenovo
preinstalls) was leaking memory through `audiodg.exe`, and an `nssm.exe` was leaking too (I never
found out which service registered that one). I removed both from the auto start lists, and later on
disabled Nahimic completely:

```powershell
Set-Service NahimicService -StartupType Disabled
```

If you want to check your own computer for leaks like these, sort your processes by memory and see
if anything has gotten way bigger than makes sense, especially a process that has been running for
days:

```powershell
Get-Process | Sort-Object PM -Descending |
  Select-Object -First 5 Name, StartTime, @{ n = 'GB'; e = { [math]::Round($_.PM / 1GB, 1) } }
```

<Step is="airflow" />

## Air

Most things were good after that. Then I wanted to use the laptop for gaming with friends and
realized things were still not the best, so I restarted (of course), 3D printed a riser for airflow,
since the Legion pulls air in from underneath, and cleaned the fins with an air gun. That did
improve things, I just couldn't tell you by how much, since I never thought of measuring any of this.

<Step is="measure" />

## nvidia-smi will tell you why your GPU is slow

I wish I had known this ten years ago: `nvidia-smi` can tell you whether your GPU is being throttled
and why.

```powershell
nvidia-smi --query-gpu=clocks_event_reasons.active,clocks.gr,temperature.gpu,power.draw --format=csv -l 2
```

`clocks_event_reasons.active` is a bitmask. `0x4` means the GPU hit its power limit (pretty normal
on a laptop), and `0x20` means the GPU is too hot and is slowing itself down. I had no idea any of
this existed, I just asked Claude Code why even a simple game like Peak was running badly, and
Claude wrote a logger around that command and had me play for nine minutes:

```
300 sec  SW POWER CAP           pinned against the 115 W ceiling
228 sec  SW THERMAL SLOWDOWN    too hot to use even that
  0 sec  unthrottled

15:19:46  load starts        65 °C
15:25:53  thermal throttle   87 °C
```

Six minutes from cold to thermal throttling, the GPU clock dropping from 1,965 MHz to as low as
1,290, and not a single second where the GPU was running unthrottled. Claude's first guess was dust
or old thermal paste, but Peak was rendering at native 4K, because the laptop is plugged into a 4K
TV and I never changed the resolution. A 115 W laptop GPU can't do 4K, so I dropped the resolution
to 1440p.

<ThrottleTrace />

<Step is="tuned" />

## Two modes

The chart is from later that night, running Subnautica 2 at 1440p. I had never played Subnautica
like that, 60 FPS with maybe a few drops, even though the GPU was thermal throttling more than half
the time. The CPU was the bad part, averaging 98.8 °C. The CPU and GPU share heatpipes in the
Legion, so a hot CPU takes cooling away from the GPU, and since games on this laptop are limited by
the GPU anyway, I capped the CPU at 35 W.

I had Claude create server mode and gaming mode scripts out of everything we learned. Server mode
keeps the laptop quiet and cool and makes sure Jellyfin and friends are running. Gaming mode
switches to the performance power plan, turns on the 15 W GPU boost, sets the fan curve and the CPU
cap, and stops whatever is competing for the disk.

Two things I didn't know before these scripts: `powercfg` doesn't change the GPU power limit, the
laptop's embedded controller does, so the scripts set the mode there and check that the mode
actually changed. And the power button was glowing red for "performance" while the GPU was still
capped at 115 W out of 130 (`nvidia-smi -q -d POWER` shows the real limit).

<Step is="collapse" />

## Twenty days later, 2 FPS

My friends and I found time to play again, so I pressed the gaming mode shortcut,
opened Subnautica, and... 2 FPS?

Normally I would have just restarted, but my wife was watching a movie on Jellyfin and I couldn't
break her stream. So I told Opus 5.5 that the game was running at 2 FPS and that I couldn't restart,
and sent Opus on a quest to figure out why.

<Step is="transcode" />

## The movie

Before I even opened the game the GPU was already at 100 W and 83 °C, the video decoder was 76%
busy, the encoder 43%, and 2.8 GB of the 8 GB of VRAM was taken.

My wife had subtitles on, and the subtitles were PGS. The Jellyfin app on the iPad can't render PGS
(unlike text subtitles like SubRip), so Jellyfin was burning the subtitles into the video, which
means decoding a 4K HDR movie, tone mapping, drawing the subtitles and re-encoding at 1080p.
Subnautica 2 is an Unreal Engine 5 game that wants most of the 8 GB of VRAM, and my best guess for
the 2 FPS is that Subnautica ran out of VRAM and spilled into system memory.

My wife was mostly listening and not really watching, so we turned the subtitles off and Jellyfin
went back to direct playing the file. If you want to check whether something is transcoding on your
GPU:

```powershell
nvidia-smi --query-gpu=utilization.encoder,utilization.decoder,memory.used --format=csv -l 2
```

The game log says I was getting 21 to 33 FPS after that. We also noticed gaming mode had only half
applied, because Windows had gone back to the Balanced power plan. With both fixed the FPS was okay,
but the game was constantly stuttering and freezing, and last time I played, Subnautica was so
smooth. None of this was making sense.

<Step is="overlay" />

## The overlay

Windows had committed 48.6 GB of a 49.9 GB limit, and 12.7 GB of that was a single NVIDIA Overlay
process that had been running for twenty days, since my last restart. (The same process was also
reporting 58 TB of GPU memory on an 8 GB card, so NVIDIA's accounting was clearly broken too.)

```powershell
Stop-Process -Id 14080   # NVIDIA Overlay
```

NVIDIA starts a new overlay process right away, and the new one uses about 150 MB. Commit went down
to 35.6 GB. I love that we found this one, a restart would have wiped the leak away and I would have
never known the overlay was leaking. The game was still stuttering though.

<Step is="kernel" />

## Into the kernel

Then I noticed the stuttering wasn't just the game. Opus's own terminal output was jumping and
stuttering too, so the problem had to be something underneath everything. The System process was
using between 1.1 and 1.4 cores nonstop.

Honestly, this is where I would have given up and restarted. I didn't even know you could get a
stack trace of the Windows kernel, but xperf from the Windows Performance Toolkit does exactly that
(as admin):

```powershell
xperf -on PROC_THREAD+LOADER+PROFILE -stackwalk Profile
Start-Sleep 10
xperf -d trace.etl

$env:_NT_SYMBOL_PATH = 'srv*C:\symbols*https://msdl.microsoft.com/download/symbols'
xperf -i trace.etl -symbols -a stack -pid 4 -butterfly 50   # pid 4 is System
```

<AnnotatedStack />

Every window, the game and the terminal included, gets drawn into GPU memory, and the Desktop Window
Manager combines those windows into the frame on your screen. A single kernel thread manages GPU
memory for all programs, and that thread was spending three quarters of its time walking a list.
While that thread is walking the list, everything that needs GPU memory has to wait, so the whole
screen was hitching and not just the game.

## 32,709 zombie processes

A zombie is a process that has exited but can't be cleaned up by Windows, because some other program
still holds a handle to the process. Opus compared the number of process objects in the kernel
against the number of processes that were running:

```
Live processes: 306   Process objects in kernel: 33,015   => zombies ~ 32,709

Name                    HandleCount
GameManagerService            33695
svchost                       29855
Razer Synapse Service         28193
```

Razer Game Manager watches every process you launch to detect games, and seems to open a handle for
every process without ever closing them. Twenty days without a restart comes out to about 1,600 new
zombies a day. The software that makes my mouse glow was slowing down every frame on my screen,
great.

The bar under `kernel` on the laptop's screen is that list drawn to scale, and the game freezes
whenever the list gets walked.

<Step is="fixed" />

```powershell
Stop-Service 'Razer Game Manager Service', 'Razer Synapse Service' -Force
```

Within a minute the zombies went from 32,709 to 260, the System process went from about 1.25 cores
to 0.2, and the stuttering was gone. No restart, and my wife didn't even notice.

</Story>

## Check yours

If you are on Windows, open PowerShell and run:

```powershell
(Get-Counter '\Objects\Processes').CounterSamples[0].CookedValue - (Get-Process).Count
```

A few hundred is normal. Thousands means some program is leaking handles, and this will most likely
show you which one:

```powershell
Get-Process | Sort-Object HandleCount -Descending | Select-Object -First 5 Name, HandleCount
```

If you run these please tell me your number and your top offender, I am really curious to see what
other people find.

## Everything is broken

So over the years the problems were Nahimic, some service running under nssm, NVIDIA's overlay and
Razer, programs that either come preinstalled or that pretty much everyone with a gaming laptop has.
Gaming mode now disables the Razer services and prints the zombie count at the end.

Everything is broken on Windows, but at least now I can diagnose the problem and fix it at the
source. "Game has low FPS" used to be a problem I had no idea how to even start on, so I would
restart and hope. If your computer has problems you don't understand, I recommend letting an agent
loose on them, you might be surprised by what the agent finds.
